/**
@page Seitz_Current_Traction_Comparison Seitz friction: current constitutive traction

@section seitz_current_scope Formulation and correction

IWG_Isotropic_Struc_Nonlinear_Contact_Seitz uses current constitutive traction
in the tangential trial, following Seitz, Wall and Popp, Computational Mechanics
63 (2019), 1091-1110, DOI: 10.1007/s00466-018-1638-x, Eq. (33):
\f[
 \{t_\tau\}_{\omega_s}
 = P_{B(\bar\mu)}\left(\{t_\tau\}_{\omega_s}
                    +\gamma_\tau\Delta u_\tau\right).
\f]
Previous configurations enter the slip increment, Eq. (16); the traction is
evaluated from the current displacement iterate. Equation (34) uses the same
current traction in the Nitsche consistency term. The paragraph following that
equation distinguishes Seitz's slip increment from the velocity used by Mlika.

The former MORIS trial used previous constitutive traction instead. The
correction changes it to current constitutive traction, including the current
stress derivative in the current Jacobian. The previous-slab Jacobian retains
only slip-kinematics derivatives. Previous-stress helpers and time-index logic
have been removed. No independent friction history is needed. Public Seitz IWG
identifiers and parameters remain the same; the temporary alternative IWG and
its factory identifiers have been removed after the controlled comparison.

This change retains MORIS's existing reference-traction, ray-mapping, penalty
and one-sided assembly conventions. It does not introduce the paper's harmonic
traction weighting or claim equivalence of every spatial term. Mlika friction
development remains on hold.

@section seitz_current_patch Independent sticking regression

The Catch filter [SeitzStickPatch] prescribes an affine deformation of two
matching Q1 plane-strain blocks meeting at y=0. Both use E=10 and nu=0.3,
with F=[[1,kappa],[0,0.98]]. The interface displacement is zero at both times,
so the gap and slip increment vanish exactly. The analytic reference for the
compressible neo-Hookean Wriggers model is P_xy=G*kappa, G=E/(2*(1+nu)).
Friction coefficient 1 keeps the selected shear values inside the friction bound.

The neutral variant eliminates test-traction terms. Partition of unity gives
leader tangential force G*kappa/2 for unit integration weight; the factor 1/2
comes from MORIS's one-sided contribution. The follower force must be equal and
opposite. Current shear takes values +/-0.01; previous shear takes 0, 0.004,
0.01 and -0.004. Loading, held stress and reversal must all transfer the current
traction independently of the previous stress. References come from the analytic
constitutive law, not from stored solver output.

Before consolidation, a separate IWG isolated the traction-time change. With
current kappa=0.01, the reference force was 0.0192307692308. The former IWG
returned 0, 0.00769230769231, 0.0192307692308 and -0.00769230769231 for the
four previous shears listed above. The corrected IWG returned the reference in
every case. The permanent regression retains the analytic check without keeping
the incorrect implementation in production code.

[SeitzTraction] checks current and previous-slab Jacobians against centered
finite differences with Q1/Q2 deformed contact geometry, neo-Hookean and
Saint-Venant--Kirchhoff models, all three theta variants, sticking, sliding,
frictionless, open and penalty-only contact. Previous configurations remain
necessary for slip kinematics; fixture traction-history ISV setup is obsolete
and has been removed.

@section seitz_current_cycle Global comparison plan

The prescribed-field patch isolates consistency; it is not a solved global
load cycle. A further physical benchmark should fix a lower elastic block,
compress the interface, and apply a tangential load cycle to an upper block.
Keep the normal preload fixed. Start below the Coulomb limit, exceed it, unload,
and reverse far enough to slide in the opposite direction. Record tangential
force, slip increment, accumulated signed slip, normal force, frictional work
and nonlinear convergence. Repeat with subdivided loading increments and refined
meshes, including a frictionless control and a held-load interval. First use
small strains and linear elasticity, then finite-strain hyperelasticity.

@section seitz_current_validation Verification after consolidation

The corrected existing IWG passed 610 assertions in 22 focused contact cases
on each of Armadillo and Eigen, using one MPI rank and one math-library thread.
Both focused builds compiled the updated test, factory and Seitz sources against
the configured backend's remaining libraries. The ordinary Armadillo CMake
INT-test and Parabolic_Indenter targets also built successfully.

All four Armadillo CTest Parabolic_Indenter-Seitz cases passed: frictionless,
frictional, frictionless sensitivity and frictional sensitivity. The sensitivity
cases compare the transient adjoint with full-workflow finite differences of a
material ADV. Existing numerical references and tolerances were retained.
A full Eigen build and Eigen end-to-end sensitivity rerun were not performed.
The independent global shear loading-cycle benchmark described above remains
future verification beyond these existing indenter checks.
*/
